The `performAttack` function was calling `setMessage` inside the `setGameState` callback, creating nested setState calls that violate React best practices. Additionally, the game lacked win condition logic despite being documented in the README.

## Changes

**State Management**
- Refactored message updates to use `useRef` + `useEffect` pattern instead of nested setState
- Eliminates race conditions from direct setState calls within state updater functions

**Win Condition**
- Added check after each attack to detect when a player controls all territories
- Displays victory message when win condition is met

**Type Safety**
- Added null validation for `winnerId` before accessing player object

## Implementation

```typescript
const pendingMessageRef = useRef<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
  if (pendingMessageRef.current) {
    setMessage(pendingMessageRef.current);
    pendingMessageRef.current = null;
  }
}, [gameState]);

const performAttack = (attacker, defender) => {
  setGameState(prev => {
    // ... attack logic ...

    // Check for win condition
    const owners = new Set(newTerritories.map(t => t.owner));
    if (owners.size === 1 && newTerritories.length > 0) {
      const winnerId = newTerritories[0].owner;
      if (winnerId != null) {
        messageToSet = `🎉 ${prev.players[winnerId].name} wins!`;
      }
    }

    if (messageToSet) {
      pendingMessageRef.current = messageToSet;
    }
    // ...
  });
};
```

